  "articleBody": "When you're patching drywall, it's easy to make a few common mistakes that can leave your wall looking less than perfect. One big one is overfilling the joint compound. You want thin, even coats; too much at once just makes more work for you later with extra sanding. On the flip side, underfilling means you won't get a smooth, level surface. Another common blunder is sanding too aggressively or not feathering the edges of your patch into the existing wall. This can leave noticeable ridges or depressions that really show up once paint is applied. And speaking of paint, using the wrong shade or failing to prime before painting can make your repair stand out like a sore thumb. Don't forget to clean up all that dust too, as it can affect how well new paint adheres. Finally, and this is a biggie, if your drywall damage came from a leak or an impact, make sure you've addressed the *cause* of the damage. Otherwise, you'll be patching the same spot again sooner than you think! If these steps sound a bit daunting, that's when a professional touch can ensure a seamless result.",
